        ":question/id": "8806c061-892c-5fd2-baf1-cd903aa5ea31",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-141089",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$ is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is true. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement”; “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” only",
